  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed breakdown of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n---\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\nWomen do have Adam's apples, but they are usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n---\n\n### Why Is It Larger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Hormonal differences:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in males and females until puberty. Once puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) in males may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n2. **Structural growth:** The structural size of the thyroid cartilage in males tends to increase during puberty, and the laryngeal protuberance becomes more anteriorly focused.\n\n3. **Shape differences:** There is also a difference in the *shape* of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n4. **Female larynx growth is smaller:** Girls also undergo changes to their voice box during puberty, but the degree of laryngeal growth in females isn't as significant as in males, so most women don't have a noticeable Adam's apple.\n\n---\n\n### Why Does Voice Pitch Connect to Adam's Apple Size?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one. This is why men's voices typically deepen significantly during puberty.\n\n---\n\n### Can Women Have a More Prominent Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple. For example, conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can cause an increase in testosterone levels in women, potentially contributing to a more prominent laryngeal prominence.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, the larger Adam's apple in men is primarily driven by the surge of **testosterone during puberty**, which causes the larynx to grow significantly larger than it does in women, pushing the thyroid cartilage outward into that characteristic visible bump.",
